WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:02.173
(audience applauds)
- [Kelly] (speaks faintly)

00:00:02.173 --> 00:00:03.006
- How are you?

00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:04.373
- I'm so good.

00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:06.360
I'm a little tired, how are you?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.338
- I'm doing well, I'm doing well.

00:00:07.338 --> 00:00:08.171
- You good?

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:09.004
- I really love that dress.

00:00:09.004 --> 00:00:10.180
- That's like the
softest sweater, as well.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.400
- You know, I had to wear
it 'cause it feels so soft

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.233
and it's still wintertime and all that.

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.773
- I know, how do you not
just keep touching your arms?

00:00:14.773 --> 00:00:15.606
- I know.

00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:16.439
Keep touching myself?

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:17.366
How do I not keeping touching myself?

00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:18.300
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.670
- Mine is soft today as well.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.030
This is our theme today.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.890
But you had a birthday, January 1st so...

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.023
- Yes, yes.
(audience cheers)

00:00:25.023 --> 00:00:25.856
- That's exciting.
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:00:25.856 --> 00:00:28.680
- So do you feel trumped by
New Year's or are you excited

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.660
'cause everyone's yelling and
cheering on your birthday.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.493
- Oh no, no.

00:00:31.493 --> 00:00:32.560
So everyone...

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.330
It's not good having a
birthday on January 1st

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.410
'cause people owe me a lot of presents

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.370
for Christmas, you know?

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.510
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.640
'Cause they'll save the Christmas presents

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.940
and just give 'em to me on
my birthday or vice versa.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.060
So I do a lot of presents,
especially from my parents.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.810
- And everyone's probably tired
on your birthday. (laughs)

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.000
- Which is cool, I'm cool with that.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:52.833
I'm cool with that.

00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:54.107
But people party, they look
forward to my birthday.

00:00:54.107 --> 00:00:56.070
'Cause people wanna go
out and have a good time.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.292
- Oh, you're a half glass
full kinda guy, I like it.

00:00:58.292 --> 00:01:00.310
- Of course, always, always.
- Okay, I like it, I like it.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.130
Well, speaking of birthdays,
so "Boyz N the Hood",

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.553
is it the 30th anniversary?

00:01:03.553 --> 00:01:04.543
(audience applauds)
- Yes, yes.

00:01:04.543 --> 00:01:06.712
- Last summer?
- Something like that, yeah.

00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:07.545
(audience cheers)
Yeah.

00:01:07.545 --> 00:01:10.570
- [Kelly] I mean the film
it just, such a pop culture.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.426
- I was only 10 (Kelly laughs)
so if you do the math...

00:01:13.426 --> 00:01:14.259
(audience laughs)

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:15.092
If you do the math it's...

00:01:15.092 --> 00:01:15.925
- Yeah!

00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:16.840
Oh my God, no but it's...

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.730
Why do you think people
resonated so much then

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:22.670
and honestly, unfortunately
still resonate with it now?

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.750
- You know, it's interesting
because first of all

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.480
you know, shout out to the
late great John Singleton.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.778
He was incredible, he was
just a great visionary.

00:01:31.778 --> 00:01:33.550
(audience applauds)

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.530
But I think a lot of the themes still

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.360
are very prevalent to this day.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.010
- Which kinda sucks.
- It does.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.280
Yeah, it really does.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.330
And then you know, he put together,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.860
it was a great script that he wrote.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.270
He directed a great movie
and he assembled you know,

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.280
a great cast of people who
were just really good people.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.460
You know?

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.870
And it was just an incredible
project to be a part of.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.930
- Yeah, I mean I can imagine,
especially as an actor,

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.820
like it's cool to be a part of something

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.850
that just hits pop culture in such a way,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.990
but also has such an important message.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:03.823
- Without a doubt.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:08.070
I mean, to think now that people
are still talking about it.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.950
When I did that movie so
many years ago I mean,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.390
I just wanted to be a part of something,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.530
I just wanted to get a job, I
just wanted to be in a movie

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.060
and there's no way I
thought that it would be,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.260
people would still be talking
about it to this very day.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.295
So it's a blessing, it's a blessing.

00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:22.660
(audience applauds)
- It's a total blessing.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.920
Well your character was
always eating in the movie,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.920
which I like dream role,
but like did you add that

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.380
or did they want you to be eating

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.213
throughout the whole thing?

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:34.710
- John came to me, he was
like, "Listen, now I want you

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.437
to always be eating something", you know?

00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:37.729
And he says, "What do you like?

00:02:37.729 --> 00:02:38.562
(Kelly laughs)
Yeah!

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:39.550
He was like, "'Cause I
want the character...",

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.430
'Cause he was a football
player, he was an athlete.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.040
So he's always just consuming food.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.130
And so they were like,
"Well, what do you like?"

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:45.987
And at the time I said,

00:02:45.987 --> 00:02:47.580
"Well, I like Chocodiles", and all that.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.910
So he said, "Okay, we'll
get you some Chocodiles

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:49.750
and some milk."

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.440
So-
- Wait what's a Chocodile?

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.900
- A Chocodile is like a chocolate Twinkie

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.330
back in the day.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.840
- What?
- Yes, yes it was like a-

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:57.740
- I've never even heard of that!

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:58.810
- Oh, you haven't heard of Chocodiles?

00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.643
- [Kelly] No!

00:02:59.643 --> 00:03:00.550
- Oh wow, wow!

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:01.383
Wow, yes.
- Like I was like,

00:03:01.383 --> 00:03:03.341
"What kind of foreign food
are you talking about?"

00:03:03.341 --> 00:03:04.174
(Morris laughs)
Like like a Chocodile?

00:03:04.174 --> 00:03:05.270
No, I never heard of it.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.410
I was a Ding Dong person,
I like Ding Dongs.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.383
- It's kinda like a Ding Dong.

00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:09.216
- Like kinda like a Ding Dong?

00:03:09.216 --> 00:03:10.049
- It's kinda like a Ding Dong.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:10.882
It's kinda like a Ding Dong.

00:03:10.882 --> 00:03:12.690
And it was, and I ate a lot of 'em.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.148
- I think you just said Ding
Dong three times. (laughs)

00:03:15.148 --> 00:03:16.274
(Morris and audience laugh)

00:03:16.274 --> 00:03:17.913
Kinda like Ding Dong, kinda
like a Ding Dong. (laughs)

00:03:17.913 --> 00:03:18.980
- Kinda like a Ding
Dong, it's kinda like a-

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:19.950
Well you know the thing about it,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.820
I really loved Chocodiles
before the movie.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:23.770
I didn't like 'em so much after.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:24.790
- [Kelly] Oh you were over it.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.211
- 'Cause I had to eat 'em so much.

00:03:26.211 --> 00:03:28.410
- (chuckles) Was it fun or intimidating

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.406
to be such a heartthrob
like right out of the gate?

00:03:30.406 --> 00:03:32.690
(Morris chuckles)

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.245
I know you probably hate that
question (audience cheers)

00:03:34.245 --> 00:03:36.807
but it's a serious question
'cause you go everywhere

00:03:36.807 --> 00:03:39.200
and people are always
yellin' you know, at you

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.936
and that's gotta be kind of,
does it make you uncomfortable?

00:03:41.936 --> 00:03:42.769
- You know, listen.

00:03:42.769 --> 00:03:46.320
So I've had certain
things like that happen.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.170
But here's the thing, so when
I first did "Boyz N the Hood"

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.847
right, before I had the
movie, I was just like,

00:03:50.847 --> 00:03:54.120
"I wanna do something that
people will recognize me for.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.410
'Cause that'll help me get other jobs."

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.630
- [Kelly] Absolutely.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.750
- So I did "Boyz N the Hood"
and now like almost every day

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.866
yeah, multiple times a
day, people yell, "Ricky!"

00:04:03.866 --> 00:04:05.232
(Kelly and audience laugh)
Right?

00:04:05.232 --> 00:04:06.065
The funny thing about that is

00:04:06.065 --> 00:04:07.850
it's like it's the first
time I've ever heard it.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:08.949
They said, "Hey, Ricky!

00:04:08.949 --> 00:04:09.782
Have you heard that before?"

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:10.615
I was like, "Yeah."

00:04:10.615 --> 00:04:12.470
- You're so original.
- Yeah exactly, right?

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:17.010
But even with that and you know,
the "label of a heartthrob"

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.390
you know, I'm just gracious
and grateful for everything

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.850
because I never forget
when I never had a job

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.460
and I started out in this industry

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.020
and it's just so tough
and it's so competitive.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.670
I just wanted to be recognized,

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.770
known for anything so I can keep working.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:32.630
And so now doing that-

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:33.987
- Pay your bills doing what you love.

00:04:33.987 --> 00:04:34.820
(audience applauds)

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.986
- Pay the bills doing what I love, right?

00:04:35.986 --> 00:04:39.930
And so now that I have that
and when I've had that,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.888
hey, they can call me
Ricky till the day I die.

00:04:41.888 --> 00:04:42.721
(audience laughs)

00:04:42.721 --> 00:04:44.096
I appreciate it you know, I love it.

00:04:44.096 --> 00:04:44.929
- Hey I'll never live down Kelly Clarkson

00:04:44.929 --> 00:04:45.990
from "40-Year-Old Virgin", so...

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.547
People do that to me all the
time and I'm always like,

00:04:47.547 --> 00:04:49.782
"I've never heard that
before, you're so unique."

00:04:49.782 --> 00:04:50.615
(Morris laughs)

00:04:50.615 --> 00:04:51.682
But I actually laugh at it every time.

00:04:51.682 --> 00:04:52.515
(audience cheers)

00:04:52.515 --> 00:04:53.478
We're back with Morris Chestnut

00:04:53.478 --> 00:04:56.729
and that was a look at his
show "Our Kind of People".

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.990
So what's the meaning behind the title?

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.900
- So the title is based on a book.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.010
It was a book called "Our Kind of People."

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.250
And it basically, it covers the lives

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.920
of some wealthy elite
people in Martha's Vineyard.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.790
And so that was the title of the show.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.190
And that's pretty much
what the show is about.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:16.140
It's about a wealthy family
in Martha's Vineyard.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.360
And it's about power and control
and sex and salaciousness

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.850
and all of that other
good stuff in between.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.157
- Well, today is MLK Day

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:27.670
so what does his legacy
mean to you specifically?

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.360
- Wow, I mean, there's
so much I couldn't even

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.193
begin to cover it.

00:05:31.193 --> 00:05:34.720
But just all his activism
and everything that he did

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:35.746
for the Civil Rights Movement.

00:05:35.746 --> 00:05:36.579
- [Kelly] Selflessness, yeah.

00:05:36.579 --> 00:05:39.560
- Selflessness and just the
human being that he was.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.480
And I'll always respect
everything that he's done,

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.850
but in particular, how he did it.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.540
He just uses articulations
and his knowledge

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.790
and this charisma as power.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.070
And I love that.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.350
- Which is true power.
- He was very effective.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.113
It's true power and he was very effective.

00:05:55.113 --> 00:05:56.930
And I just truly, I
mean I can't say enough.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.290
I mean, we can go on for
this for about five minutes.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.350
- I know that hard to
wrap it up in a synopsis.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.290
Yeah, now that I've asked the question.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.430
- But no, but it's you know,

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.050
it's everything that he stands for.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.510
And even unfortunately, I mean you know,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.020
even if we listened to his speech today,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.215
we you know, back then-

00:06:09.215 --> 00:06:10.048
- [Kelly] Still relevant.

00:06:10.048 --> 00:06:11.209
- Still relevant.

00:06:11.209 --> 00:06:13.630
Still relevant and we
still have a ways to go.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:14.490
- Amen.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.490
Well, you visited the motel
where Dr. King was assassinated.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.590
What was that like for you?

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.370
- I was there and I said, "Oh
yeah, I wanna go by there."

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:27.110
Just to be there and just
to look at the layout

00:06:28.256 --> 00:06:30.740
of everything from afar and-

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.010
- It had to feel intense.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.010
- It was definitely intense.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.890
It was definitely intense
to know that he was there

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.370
and everything that's happened there.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:39.090
And it was just something
I just had to see

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.240
and I was glad that I was there

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:41.240
and I was glad that I saw it.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.300
And I took a picture of it, of course.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:44.950
And I have it up on my wall.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.210
And it's just one of those things to where

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.450
it's just a moment in time
to know that I was there

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:54.170
where such a powerful,
effective person was.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:55.580
And it was very powerful, very powerful.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.400
- Yeah, I love that.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:58.880
Well, what's something
that you've been able

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.380
to share with your kids
about Dr. King's impact.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:04.410
- Oh, oh, everything about
you know, we've had definitely

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:05.630
many discussions about that.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.070
But one of the things that you know,

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.970
I tried to teach my son
when he was younger,

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:13.690
it was just to you know, to use his voice,

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.360
to use his articulation as power.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.780
But that he has an opportunity
to affect change as well.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.860
So around that time he was very young,

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.860
but it was the first time
Barack Obama was you know,

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.130
he was running for president.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.187
So I brought my son in
the ballot booth with me.

00:07:33.187 --> 00:07:34.300
- [Kelly] Oh, that's special!

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.600
- Yes, I brought him in
the ballot booth with me

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.080
and you know, he saw me and I
put his hand and we you know,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.210
we selected Barack Obama that year.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.241
And I know that may be
private to people but I did.

00:07:43.241 --> 00:07:44.400
- [Kelly] That's so important!

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.431
- Yeah, no no so it-

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:46.790
- No, it's so important to
show your kids like physically,

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.580
'cause it's one thing to tell
them what they should do,

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.030
but to actually physically do it with them

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.600
and show how important it is.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:52.433
- Oh, without a doubt.

00:07:52.433 --> 00:07:55.340
And I felt it was gonna
be a moment in history.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:56.841
I mean, it was the first black president.

00:07:56.841 --> 00:07:57.737
- [Kelly] You were right.

00:07:57.737 --> 00:07:58.570
Yeah.
- Yeah exactly, right?

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.130
It's the first black president
ever and my son was there

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.790
with me to feel that, to
feel the power of his voice,

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.150
the power of him as an American citizen.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.650
Being able to affect
change the way that he did.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.490
- And how different for your
kid, like representation.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.550
Like speaking of that,
like he will know that

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.991
like his whole life, like
that's a norm for him now.

00:08:16.991 --> 00:08:17.824
(audience applauds)
- Exactly, exactly.

00:08:17.824 --> 00:08:18.920
- You know what I'm saying?
- Yeah, exactly.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:20.920
- Like for him it's so cool because

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.850
like for us, it wasn't a norm growing up.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:25.280
And now it's like he's growing
up in a different world

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.720
and what a beautiful thing.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.100
- Yeah oh, without a
doubt, without a doubt.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.250
And then about five minutes later,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:30.301
he wanted to go play PlayStation.

00:08:30.301 --> 00:08:31.700
(Kelly and audience laugh)
But other than that...

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:32.864
- And then he was like,
"That was great dad,

00:08:32.864 --> 00:08:33.697
we checked the box." (chuckles)

00:08:33.697 --> 00:08:34.960
- Exactly, "We checked the box,
let's go play PlayStation."

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:37.810
So, but I think it means much
more to him now of course

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:39.500
than it did then.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.680
But for him to know that he was there.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:41.570
- No, that's special.
(audience cheers)

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.700
That's really cool that you did that.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.023
Well, your mom was a teacher,
my mom was a teacher as well.

00:08:46.023 --> 00:08:47.440
But she was a teacher in
South Central LA, right?

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.273
- Yes, yes.

00:08:48.273 --> 00:08:49.800
- So how did that impact you growing up?

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:50.633
- Wow.

00:08:50.633 --> 00:08:53.321
I mean, I've learned
so much from my mother.

00:08:53.321 --> 00:08:54.154
(audience croons)
And especially...

00:08:54.154 --> 00:08:55.330
Oh wow, yeah, that's her.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:56.890
I love my mother.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.250
Just from an early age I mean,
there were so many things

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:01.780
that she used to tell me that's
still stuck with me today.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:02.897
And she used to always tell me,

00:09:02.897 --> 00:09:04.260
"The early bird catches the worm!"

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.177
And she would wake me
up early on a Saturday.

00:09:06.177 --> 00:09:07.017
"Oh, I don't wanna get up."

00:09:07.017 --> 00:09:09.240
"The early bird catches the worm!"

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.450
What was interesting was,
even though my mother

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:15.010
was a teacher it took me
a while because of course,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:18.000
when I was in elementary
school and junior high school,

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.500
I would never think of my
teachers having a family.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:23.333
We kind of would just think that,

00:09:23.333 --> 00:09:25.390
"Okay, after I leave school,
they just go in the closet

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:26.223
and wait until we get back the next day."

00:09:26.223 --> 00:09:27.749
You know what I'm saying?
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:09:27.749 --> 00:09:28.880
- They just hang out here.
- They just hang out there!

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.330
And then I never thought
about that until really,

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.580
even though my mother was a teacher,

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.740
I never thought about it till I was older.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.210
Just reflecting back on all
of her sacrifices for me.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:43.630
- Boy, all teachers!
- And when she had to-

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:44.520
All teachers, right?

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.530
All teachers sacrifice...

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:46.494
Yeah because they really have

00:09:46.494 --> 00:09:49.010
to deal with us bad
students or good students.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:51.200
- And the mean, puberty and then they

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.850
don't get paid enough for that.

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:54.395
- And then they definitely
don't get paid enough.

00:09:54.395 --> 00:09:55.228
(audience applauds)
- [Kelly] No.

00:09:55.228 --> 00:09:58.640
- They definitely do not get paid enough.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.820
And so yeah, so I truly
appreciate you know, my mother,

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:02.653
everything she's done for me.

00:10:02.653 --> 00:10:05.730
And all teachers, all
teachers across the world.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.830
- They're angels.
- Yes, without a doubt.

